The test calls `fake.tar(uncompressed_size=10000, num_files=4, min_file_size=1000)` with default `compression=None`. In `Provider.tar`, those parameters are defined at function entry and then used in the validation checks, in `remaining_size = uncompressed_size`, and in the loop `for file_number in range(1, num_files + 1)`. Inside the loop, `file_number` is read when building `filename`, computing `max_allowed_size`, and checking `if file_number < num_files`; `max_allowed_size` is then read by `randint`. For the first three iterations the `if` branch defines `file_size` at line 337 and redefines `remaining_size` at line 338; on the last iteration the `else` branch defines `file_size` at line 340 from the current `remaining_size`. `filename` is later read by `TarInfo(name=filename)`, and `file_size` is read either by the subtraction updating `remaining_size` or by `self.generator.binary(file_size)`. Collecting the unique observed def-use pairs from that executed path gives the following set.

